## What do you think separates a Senior Software Engineer role from a Software Engineer?

Scope of ownership and judgment, not years or syntax. A software engineer is given a problem and solves it well. A senior engineer steps back first. Is this the right problem, and which parts are better left alone? What does it affect downstream, and who feels it in six months? Seniority is the altitude you operate at, not the lines you write.

That wider view shows up in concrete ways. First, judgment under ambiguity, knowing when to ship lean and carry a little debt, and when it will hurt later. Second, ownership past the merge, the tests, docs, on-call, and production reality, not just the pull request. Third, multiplying the people around you through review and design input, leaving the next engineer's job easier, not just finishing your own.

The common thread is accountability for outcomes, not just output. A senior engineer keeps one eye on the task and one on the broader system, the business, and the people it affects, trusted to make that call without being told.
